<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jugurtha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 160–104 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jugurtha was king of Numidia, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> who seized the whole kingdom after killing one adoptive brother and fighting the other. His massacre of Italian traders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> in 112 BCE led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurthine-war">Jugurthine War</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which exposed corruption in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>. Betrayed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> in 105 BCE by his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-i">Bocchus I</a> of Mauretania, he was led in Marius's triumph and died in prison in Rome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jugurtha.jpg" alt="Jugurtha" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faustus_Cornelius_Sulla,_AR_denarius,_56_BC,_RRC_426-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
